"""Louisiana state content: lessons and practice questions.

Louisiana splits the salesperson examination and reports SCALED SCORES that
are explicitly NOT percentages. The Pearson VUE Louisiana Real Estate and
Appraisers Candidate Handbook: National 80 items / 150 minutes; State 55
items / 90 minutes; both 135 items / 240 minutes. The handbook sets the
passing score at a SCALED 70 on the national portion and a SCALED 70 on the
state portion for salespersons (brokers and prelicensing instructors need 75
on each), and states that the scaled score "is neither the number of
questions you answered correctly nor the percentage of questions you
answered correctly". So this pack sets pass_published to False and carries
a note, as NY, PA, WI, WA and IN do.

Louisiana is the one CIVIL LAW state, and the exam reflects it: a full
13-15 items of the 55 are the Louisiana Civil Law System, and another 13-15
are the Louisiana Law of Agency. Nothing about immovables, usufruct,
servitudes, community property, lesion beyond moiety, redhibition,
reconduction or acquisitive prescription carries over from a common-law
state. Do not reason by analogy here.

Louisiana's agency law is also unusual: DESIGNATED AGENCY IS PRESUMED. A
licensee working with a person represents that person as a designated agent
unless there is a written agreement saying otherwise or the licensee is
performing only ministerial acts.

Every rule below was read on 2026-11-12 from the official text:
  - La. R.S. 37:1430 to 37:1478, the Louisiana Real Estate License Law, as
    published by the Louisiana Real Estate Commission at lrec.gov.
  - La. R.S. 9:3891 to 9:3899, Agency Relations in Real Estate
    Transactions, as published by the Commission at lrec.gov.
  - The Louisiana Civil Code, from legis.la.gov.
  - The Pearson VUE Louisiana Real Estate and Appraisers Candidate
    Handbook, for the examination table, the scaled passing scores and the
    55-item state content outline effective September 1, 2021.
The section a fact comes from is cited next to it. Do not add a fact here
without reading its source first.
"""

